I propose to examine the federal science funding crisis through this question: to what extent can Congress protect basic research when a president seeks to reduce or redirect its funding? I will emphasize physics, astrophysics, and cosmology, using the National Science Foundation (NSF) as the principal case and NASA as a comparison. My tentative argument is that congressional appropriations are necessary but insufficient for stability: executive decisions about agency priorities and grant administration can also affect whether research commitments endure.

The essay will first establish the historical context, from Vannevar Bush's 1945 argument for public support of basic research to the creation of NSF in 1950 and the Cold War expansion of science. The original disagreement over scientific independence and presidential accountability provides a useful comparison with today's disputes @mazuzan. I will also examine the CHIPS and Science Act, distinguishing its funding authorizations from the appropriations needed to deliver that support.

The central case will be the fiscal year 2026 budget conflict. President Trump's request proposed \$3.9 billion for NSF, presenting the allocation as a response to fiscal constraints and national priorities @nsfrequest2025. Congress subsequently approved a science funding bill defended by Senators Susan Collins and Jerry Moran on competitiveness and research grounds @senate2026. I will compare presidential requests, congressional allocations, and agency implementation rather than treating proposed cuts as completed reductions. The essay will then consider how the fiscal year 2027 proposals extend this dispute.

My evidence will include agency budget documents, appropriations legislation, congressional hearings, and scholarship on science policy. Statements from universities and scientific associations will help assess their influence as interest groups, while grant and program data will test claims about consequences for researchers. I will examine both national arguments and legislators' interests in research institutions within their constituencies.

Finally, I will assess the counterargument that elected officials must be able to redirect spending toward urgent needs and control deficits. My provisional position favors predictable support for basic research, coupled with transparent review and congressional oversight. I will evaluate multiyear funding and reporting requirements against fiscal flexibility and democratic accountability. This approach connects the funding crisis to the course's themes of separation of powers, bureaucracy, interest groups, and public policy.


